"><para.text date.updated="0">Canada, Pan-Canadian Framework on Clean Growth and Climate Change, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb3b2c05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.canada.ca/en/services/environment/weather/climatechange/pan-canadian-framework.html</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Under the Paris Agreement, Canada committed to reducing its emissions to 30&percnt; below 2005 levels by 2030 and to take steps for adapting to climate change impacts.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Canada ratified the Paris Agreement on October 5th, 2016 and it entered into force on November 4th, 2016.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba&apos;s initial refusal to sign the <ital>Pan-Canadian Framework</ital> related to Manitoba&apos;s objection to the federal government&apos;s carbon pricing plan (discussed further in <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:18">&sect;8:18</ref></cite> and <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:19">&sect;8:19</ref></cite> of this chapter).<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For additional discussion see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb3d9d25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Office of the Auditor General, Manitoba, Managing Climate Change (a follow-up to our December 2010 audit) (October 2017), at p.10</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb400e05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.oag.mb.ca/audit-reports/report/managing-climate-change-a-follow-up-to-our-december-2010-audit/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Note that in April 2021, the Prime Minister of Canada announced a new national emissions reduction target <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;13" nep=";;15" hyphenated="n" folio="8-13" backFolio="8-14" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>of 40-45&percnt; below 2005 levels by 2030.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">14</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb400e15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Canada, Prime Minister Trudeau Announces Increased Climate Ambition (April 22, 2021)</ref></cite> available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb400e25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://pm.gc.ca/en/news/news-releases/2021/04/22/prime-minister-trudeau-announces-increased-climate-ambition</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> This was followed by the federal <ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital> which received Royal Assent in June 2021 and enshrined in legislation, the new 2030 reduction target as Canada&apos;s Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) under the Paris Agreement as well as a commitment to revise the national target every five years in order to achieve net-zero greenhouse gas emissions in Canada by 2050.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>Canadian Net-Zero Emissions Accountability Act</ital>, S.C. 2021, c. 22 at ss. 1.1(2), 4 and 6.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><